{"name":"Hinting Blue","hex":"#ced9dd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hinting","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of giving a hint or suggestion.","The process of adding hints to a computer-based font in order to align the glyphs to a rasterized grid at specific sizes in a visually appealing manner."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of hint"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hinting"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Font hinting","blocks":[{"text":"Font hinting, also known as instructing, is the use of mathematical instructions to adjust the display of an outline font so that it lines up with a rasterized grid. At low screen resolutions, hinting is critical for producing clear, legible text. It can be accompanied by antialiasing and subpixel rendering for further clarity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Font-hinting-example.png/330px-Font-hinting-example.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Font_hinting","wikidata":"Q1493777"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Hinting_sample_noaa_nohint_large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Hinting_sample_noaa_nohint_large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHinting_sample_noaa_nohint_large.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Hinting_sample_aa_nohint_large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Hinting_sample_aa_nohint_large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHinting_sample_aa_nohint_large.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Hinting_sample_noaa_hint_large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Hinting_sample_noaa_hint_large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHinting_sample_noaa_hint_large.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Hinting_sample_aa_hint_large.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parse